<?xpacket begin="ï»¿" id="W5M0MpCehiHzreSzNTczkc9d"?>
<x:xmpmeta xmlns:x="adobe:ns:meta/" x:xmptk="XMP Core 6.0.0">
	<rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#">
		<rdf:Description rdf:about=""
				xmlns:pdf="http://ns.adobe.com/pdf/1.3/"
				xmlns:xmp="http://ns.adobe.com/xap/1.0/"
				xmlns:xmpMM="http://ns.adobe.com/xap/1.0/mm/"
				xmlns:xmpRights="http://ns.adobe.com/xap/1.0/rights/"
				xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"
				xmlns:pdfaExtension="http://www.aiim.org/pdfa/ns/extension/"
				xmlns:pdfaSchema="http://www.aiim.org/pdfa/ns/schema#"
				xmlns:pdfaProperty="http://www.aiim.org/pdfa/ns/property#"
				xmlns:pdfaid="http://www.aiim.org/pdfa/ns/id/">
			<pdf:Producer>MicrosoftÂ® Word for Microsoft 365</pdf:Producer>
			<pdf:Keywords>ban the box, statistical discrimination, fair-chance hiring, employment, difference-in-differences, criminal record</pdf:Keywords>
			<xmp:CreatorTool>MicrosoftÂ® Word for Microsoft 365</xmp:CreatorTool>
			<xmp:CreateDate>2026-08-19T13:08:27+03:00</xmp:CreateDate>
			<xmp:ModifyDate>2026-08-19T13:10:50+03:00</xmp:ModifyDate>
			<xmpMM:DocumentID>uuid:CFEA25E7-965B-4D3C-BCA3-8DD717F6D295</xmpMM:DocumentID>
			<xmpMM:InstanceID>uuid:CFEA25E7-965B-4D3C-BCA3-8DD717F6D295</xmpMM:InstanceID>
			<xmpRights:Marked>True</xmpRights:Marked>
			<xmpRights:WebStatement>https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/</xmpRights:WebStatement>
			<dc:creator>
				<rdf:Seq>
					<rdf:li>Nooa von SchÃ¶neman</rdf:li>
				</rdf:Seq>
			</dc:creator>
			<dc:description>
				<rdf:Alt>
					<rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Numerous states and sub-regions in the U.S. have adopted policies that aim to postpone when and if employers can access the criminal histories of applicants. These â€œBan the Boxâ€ laws are designed to help individuals with criminal records get into the labor force but have been met with concerns that removing the ability to provide a signal of a clean record could harm minority candidates if employers began substituting the missing information with observable proxies like race. Prior research done on the subject contains mixed results, with some finding evidence of statistical discrimination and others not. This thesis expands on the work of Burton and Wasser (2025) by examining the employment outcomes of young, lowly educated minority men following the implementation of Ban the Box laws using American Community Survey data for the years 2004-2019. The results show no robust employment effects of Ban the Box laws for any of the demographics, complementing the findings of Burton and Wasser (2025).</rdf:li>
				</rdf:Alt>
			</dc:description>
			<dc:rights>
				<rdf:Alt>
					<rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Copyright Â©2026 Nooa von SchÃ¶neman&#xD;&#xA;This work is licensed under a Creative Commons â€œAttribution-NonCommercial-&#xD;&#xA;ShareAlike 4.0 Internationalâ€ (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0) license.</rdf:li>
				</rdf:Alt>
			</dc:rights>
			<dc:title>
				<rdf:Alt>
					<rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Evaluating â€œFair Chanceâ€ Employment: Revisiting Ban the Boxâ€™s Effect on Minority Employment with Extended ACS Data</rdf:li>
				</rdf:Alt>
			</dc:title>
			<dc:subject>
				<rdf:Bag>
					<rdf:li>ban the box</rdf:li>
					<rdf:li>statistical discrimination</rdf:li>
					<rdf:li>fair-chance hiring</rdf:li>
					<rdf:li>employment</rdf:li>
					<rdf:li>difference-in-differences</rdf:li>
					<rdf:li>criminal record</rdf:li>
				</rdf:Bag>
			</dc:subject>
